Subject: Legacy RegExp static properties (RegExp.$1-$9, input, lastMatch, lastParen, leftContext, rightContext)

What this does
  Implements the legacy RegExp features proposal (Annex B in engines):
   - %RegExp% gets the accessors input/$_ (get and set), lastMatch/$&, lastParen/$+, leftContext/$`, rightContext/$' and $1-$9 (get only), non-enumerable and configurable.
   - Getters throw a TypeError unless the receiver is the getter's realm's %RegExp%, or after the statics were invalidated. The input setter stores ToString(value).
   - Every successful match (js_regexp_match, which every exec, test, search, split, match and replace path and the compiled replace-length path call) records in the realm (JSContext) the subject string (re_subject, one counted reference, taken only when the subject changes), the group count and a fixed copy of the capture pointers of the match and $1..$9 plus the last group's (re_legacy_cap, re_legacy_last). The getters turn them into substrings on demand. RegExp.input is the subject until it is assigned (re_input).
   - A match by a regexp made by a subclass, or by a regexp of another realm, invalidates them instead (UpdateLegacyRegExpStaticProperties / InvalidateLegacyRegExpStaticProperties).
   - A RegExp records its realm and whether its realm's %RegExp% made it ([[Realm]], [[LegacyFeaturesEnabled]]): JSRegExp's borrowed metadata pointer becomes a 32-bit offset into the bytecode, which leaves room for a 31-bit realm id (a per-context counter) and the flag, so JSRegExp and the object union keep their size.
   - RegExp.prototype.compile throws a TypeError for a regexp of another realm or a subclass instance.
   - test262.conf runs the legacy-regexp feature.

Why we need it
  JetStream 3 Basic and WSL read RegExp.$1 etc. after a match and failed without them. Every other engine has them.

Soundness
  Initial values are empty strings, as in V8 and SpiderMonkey. A failed match leaves the statics unchanged, as RegExpBuiltinExec does. Fast paths that run several matches in one operation (global match/replace loops, split) record each success in order, so the values after the operation, and inside a replace callback, are the last match's. The realm is compared by id; a context's id is unique among the first 2^31 contexts. The subject string is held (counted) by the realm until a match on another string, and released in JS_FreeContext, so the capture pointers stay valid. The copy reads 20 capture slots: every capture buffer has at least JS_REGEXP_CAPTURE_STACK_SIZE (64) entries (static_assert), and slots at or past the group count are never read.

Cost
  Octane regexp fixture, fixed work (6 runs of RegExpBenchmark, about 1.15M matches): stack VM 1.4276 -> 1.4263 G cycles:u (+1.1% instructions), register VM 0.9996 -> 1.0158 G (+1.6%; +1.9% instructions).

